update public.questions q
set explanation = v.e
from (values
('d8981319-85f2-40de-a929-1d2aca48de98', 'Record locking stops two transactions from changing the same record at the same time. Without it, one update can overwrite another and leave the data inconsistent.'),
('d8c27f8c-9bf1-4eb3-8502-7309902e37c6', 'The expected value of the sample mean equals the population mean μ, so the sample mean is an unbiased estimator of μ. The σ²/n option is the variance of the sample mean, a different quantity.'),
('d971d33a-e5d3-4f83-81f3-3ed3787e1cce', 'Expand the brackets: (2 + 3i)(1 − i) = 2 − 2i + 3i − 3i². Collect the terms and use i² = −1 to get 2 + i + 3 = 5 + i.'),
('dbc71c9d-1ac2-4b7f-bb16-754f0ea00200', 'E(X) = Σ x·P(x) = 0 × 0.2 + 1 × 0.5 + 2 × 0.3 = 0 + 0.5 + 0.6 = 1.1. The 0.6 option is only the last term and leaves out the middle one.'),
('dc38d07c-3251-4b44-954b-02fc39812bbc', 'Hooke''s law gives F = kx = 100 N/m × 0.05 m = 5 N. The 50 N option reads the extension as 0.5 m instead of 0.05 m.'),
('dc93d72b-ef77-4a7b-ab3c-219e9b528427', 'Centripetal acceleration is always directed toward the centre of the circle. It changes the direction of the velocity without changing its speed.'),
('dcc53daa-02d5-421c-b1dd-2fa97bdaf7b7', 'For calls arriving at a constant rate the expected count scales with the time interval. Over 30 minutes (0.5 hours) the expected count is 4 × 0.5 = 2. The 8 option is the count for two hours.'),
('dd36f6aa-a35c-469e-98ba-ccde61234df8', 'The standard normal curve is symmetric about 0, so the area to the left of −z equals the area to the right of z. That area is 1 − Φ(z).'),
('ddd68cc7-3b5f-49bf-8851-fa9b56ae2e19', 'Split the modulus into two equations: 2x − 1 = 5 gives x = 3, and 2x − 1 = −5 gives x = −2. Both values satisfy the original equation.'),
('df7ee9e8-e409-4bc9-8bb1-82a0d82d5fd0', 'The nth term of a GP is aʳⁿ⁻¹. For n = 6 this gives u₆ = 3 × 2⁵ = 3 × 32 = 96. The 192 option uses 2⁶ instead of 2⁵.'),
('dfe2b8d9-5308-4f02-bd38-aaf5eba18041', 'A stack returns the most recently added item first, so it is last in, first out (LIFO). A queue is the opposite: first in, first out.'),
('e05ced60-362f-4399-8d25-eab606f953bc', 'Linear speed is v = ωr. With ω = π rad s⁻¹ and r = 0.40 m, v = π × 0.40 ≈ 1.26 m s⁻¹. The 0.13 option divides by π instead of multiplying.'),
('e25797b6-9baf-4ec1-a18e-f6a586e17fe6', 'The gain in gravitational potential energy is Ep = mgh = 2 kg × 10 N/kg × 5 m = 100 J. The 10 J option leaves out g, the gravitational field strength.'),
('e2d46a3d-98d0-40e4-be1f-f196d6690cac', 'Weight is W = mg = 5 kg × 10 N/kg = 50 N. Weight is a force, measured in newtons, so the answer must be in newtons rather than kilograms.'),
('e3973690-c546-40d8-ac9c-195a02b759e7', 'Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]², so the square of the mean is subtracted from E(X²). That gives 8 − 2² = 8 − 4 = 4.'),
('e425fa01-5256-4796-b9df-9c6eb4256ed9', 'The double-angle identity is sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x. So the product 2 sin x cos x combines into the single function sin 2x. The cos 2x option is a different identity.'),
('e4c42252-76a3-4a2d-9f70-d650f00ab6cf', 's = ut + ½at² contains s, u, a and t but not v. Because v does not appear, the equation can be used when the final velocity is the quantity you do not know.'),
('e5d2cb53-2a83-466f-ac16-3bc0f13ab3f3', 'The elastic energy stored in a spring is E = ½kx², which is the area under the linear force–extension graph. The kx option is a force, not an energy.'),
('e66fef7f-9cde-41cd-81ed-f13236c61b49', 'With an early-exit check, bubble sort makes one full pass on an already sorted list, finds no swaps and stops. One pass of n − 1 comparisons is O(n).'),
('e6a36289-b7cc-40bc-8db5-9887f1616fd7', 'The horizontal component is u cos θ = 20 × cos 30° = 20 × 0.866 ≈ 17.3 m s⁻¹. The sine of the angle gives the vertical component instead.'),
('e6ad7bf4-96dc-433a-8c16-78a3c6cbf716', 'Differentiate both sides implicitly: 2x + 2y(dy/dx) = 0. Solving for dy/dx gives −2x/2y = −x/y.'),
('e6e6f187-fafa-4ba7-a2a9-a21db1b11e08', 'P(X = 3) = ⁴C₃ × 0.5³ × 0.5¹ = 4 × 0.0625 = 0.25. The 0.0625 option is the probability of one specific sequence, without the combinations factor.'),
('e783c514-4bd4-4f77-afcc-a7f83a1a4f60', 'dy/dx = 2x, so at x = 2 the gradient is 4. The line through (2, 4) with gradient 4 is y − 4 = 4(x − 2), which simplifies to y = 4x − 4.'),
('e863c2e6-52d5-4834-9347-98e4fd0986b0', 'A force acting through the pivot has zero perpendicular distance, so its moment about that pivot is zero. Taking moments about the support with the unknown reaction removes that reaction from the equation.'),
('e8b2cde3-afad-4509-be1a-d02e0597cbcb', 'a = F/m = 10 N ÷ 2 kg = 5 m s⁻². The 20 m s⁻² option multiplies the force by the mass instead of dividing.'),
('e943539a-d2c0-4517-9819-ae3d7f7ff3c1', 'A compiler analyses the whole source program before it produces object code, so it reports the errors together after the analysis step, not one at a time during execution.'),
('ec59f6ef-724d-4773-a7f8-270260923f38', 'The control unit fetches, decodes and executes instructions. Decoding means interpreting the opcode to work out which operation the instruction asks for.'),
('eddccd5d-8ad0-47a9-98ff-9c2230fa1757', 'Split the modulus: 3x − 2 ≥ 4 gives x ≥ 2, and 3x − 2 ≤ −4 gives x ≤ −2/3. The answer is x ≤ −2/3 or x ≥ 2.'),
('ef8e35e8-608e-4dab-a2f6-32521bdd38a8', 'Product rule: d/dx(x e^x) = x · e^x + 1 · e^x = e^x(x + 1). The x e^x option forgets the derivative of the x factor.'),
('efe8e30d-c860-411c-b4fa-ec50863779e5', 'The force is perpendicular to the lever arm, so the moment is F × d = 20 N × 0.5 m = 10 N m. The 20 N m option ignores the distance altogether.'),
('f01afccc-0910-4fe6-aadb-877c861dd86a', 'The exterior angles of a regular polygon add up to 360°, so each one is 360° ÷ 5 = 72°. The 108° option is the interior angle, which is 180° − 72°.'),
('f08b804b-3953-4bd7-b06e-bd8d093c964d', 'Strain is the extension divided by the original length. Both quantities are lengths, so the units cancel and strain has no unit.'),
('f180111e-9897-4797-897e-5b486ca38b2a', 'Hubble''s law gives v = H₀ × d = 70 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹ × 100 Mpc = 7000 km s⁻¹. The 700 km/s option is one tenth of this value.'),
('f1a370a1-fb17-4b96-be4f-9d61a46cdd76', 'A negative index means the reciprocal: 2⁻⁴ = 1/2⁴ = 1/16. The −16 option wrongly applies a minus sign to the result.'),
('f2645ca1-a6a1-4ac0-babe-488dd0cccbc9', 'In N(μ, σ²) the 4 is the variance, so σ = 2 for the sample mean. Z = (51 − 50)/2 = 0.5, and P(Z > 0.5) = 1 − 0.6915 = 0.3085.'),
('f2773411-71d8-4d20-9dcc-1739d1386b4b', 'When the wire is at right angles to the field, F = BIL = 0.5 T × 2 A × 0.3 m = 0.3 N. The 0.03 N option has the decimal point in the wrong place.'),
('f30c9c33-8652-4308-bfb3-f0f65da65a59', 'Indirect addressing treats the operand as the address of a location that holds another address. LDI 100 first reads the word at address 100, then loads the value at the address it contains.'),
('f370bca7-791a-4264-a5b0-c34ee3d0d00c', 'From the standard exact values, cos 60° = 1/2 = 0.5. The √3/2 value belongs to cos 30°, so the two are easy to mix up.'),
('f3b8607b-d45f-40d7-b3a6-e664764cef94', 'For −5, start with +5 = 00000101. Invert the bits to get 11111010, then add 1 to get 11111011.'),
('f3e88037-b9c7-4270-8b7f-91c118f344d8', 'Distance in parsecs is d = 1/p, where p is the parallax in arcseconds. So d = 1/0.1 = 10 pc.'),
('f41ab2f0-97b2-4ba9-973f-f0afe6844a68', 'sin 30° = ½ is one of the standard exact values. √3/2 is cos 30° and √2/2 is sin 45°, so the options are easy to confuse.'),
('f499b292-33bc-4f0c-993e-32c07c969266', 'During training a neural network adjusts its weights, usually through backpropagation with gradient descent, to reduce the error on the training data.'),
('f550454f-a250-4d08-bef7-4b2e2d1c8e43', 'The sum to infinity is S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 8/(1/2) = 16. The 8 option is the first term alone.'),
('f576e373-bcda-4299-9790-aec1e53cda38', 'Convert £4.20 to 420p so both quantities use pence: 60 : 420. Dividing both parts by 60 gives 1 : 7.'),
('f5a1f21c-075d-4448-a04e-dbff3a11f5d3', 'Add 3 to both sides to get 4x = 20. Divide both sides by 4 to get x = 5.'),
('f5b3bf13-7a3d-4e74-9ee1-9a65d6d05722', 'Substitute x = 4: f(4) = 3 × 4 − 2 = 12 − 2 = 10. The 12 option forgets the −2.'),
('f5e2dc54-f83e-46da-b473-84f8fc8ced53', 'A wider data bus carries more bits in each transfer, so more data moves per clock cycle. It does not change the number of registers or the clock speed.'),
('f655a2d9-0f2c-4a9c-888f-a47321e30530', 'Moment = F × d⊥ = 20 N × 0.30 m = 6.0 N m, using the perpendicular distance from the bolt. The 60 N m option reads 0.30 m as 3.0 m.'),
('f664a683-528e-47b0-9257-1e679725b672', 'Parallel vectors point in the same or opposite directions, so one is a scalar multiple of the other. Perpendicular vectors have a zero dot product instead.'),
('f8cc7671-07f6-4de7-ac30-3e22d0d40a97', 'Newton''s second law in momentum form is F = Δp/Δt, the rate of change of momentum. Multiplying Δp by Δt gives impulse, not force.'),
('f91541d4-44a6-4cb5-ab7c-8ef58c1d5987', 'Malus'' law gives I = I₀ cos²θ for plane-polarised light passing through a second polariser at angle θ. The I₀/2 option applies to unpolarised light entering the first polariser.'),
('f9f04174-8062-481a-8647-95a073a1570e', 'a = F/m = 12 N ÷ 4 kg = 3 m s⁻². The 0.75 option divides the mass by the force instead.'),
('fb07c6cb-bdbe-4fc9-8840-534e55da2232', 'Clustering groups data points by similarity without any labelled answers to learn from. That is what makes it unsupervised learning.'),
('feb71339-4ced-491e-93e6-2e6e07568405', 'Arc length s = rθ, so θ = s/r = 10/4 = 2.5 radians. The 40 option multiplies the arc length by the radius instead of dividing.'),
('fed4683b-d2b6-48e9-99d3-89ed7fbfb347', 'One full revolution is 360°, which is 2π radians. So π/2 is a quarter turn and π is a half turn.'),
('ff179eb6-3e5c-4b28-988c-a49efa8179a7', 'If every value is multiplied by 3, the mean is also multiplied by 3. The spread scales too, but the question asks about the mean.')
) as v(id, e)
where q.id = v.id::uuid;

update public.questions
set options = replace(options::text, 'scalarm multiples', 'scalar multiples')::jsonb
where id = 'f664a683-528e-47b0-9257-1e679725b672';
